Keep the packet loop alive across recoverable socket events. The loop ends on
any negative return from its wait helper, and three ordinary conditions reach
that path: poll()/select() interrupted by a signal (EINTR), a wake-up pipe read
that finds the byte already drained (EAGAIN) or is interrupted, and a datagram
recvmsg() that fails transiently -- an ICMP-induced ECONNREFUSED, a spurious
readable event, ENOBUFS. None of them invalidate the sockets, but each one ends
the thread: picoquic_packet_loop_poll returns -1, the caller sets ret = -1, and
the loop closes its sockets and exits with thread_is_ready cleared.

An application that keeps writing never learns of it. In a process with several
threads and timers -- OBS, for example -- a signal lands in poll() within the
first minute, and from that moment the connection sends nothing: no data, no
ACKs, no keep-alive. The peer sees a connection that went silent and reaps it
on its idle timer, while the publisher still believes it is streaming.

An interrupted wait is reported as a timeout (re-arm and wait again), an
interrupted or empty pipe read serves the wake it signalled, and a transient
datagram error skips that read. Everything else keeps the previous behaviour.

Applies to picoquic 55b473e207e436d06ea9a2895cc1fc555d42c81c.

diff --git a/picoquic/sockloop.c b/picoquic/sockloop.c
--- a/picoquic/sockloop.c
+++ b/picoquic/sockloop.c
@@ -1718,8 +1718,16 @@
     *action = picoquic_packet_loop_action_none;
 
     if (ret_poll < 0) {
-        bytes_recv = -1;
-        DBG_PRINTF("Error: poll returns %d\n", ret_poll);
+        if (errno == EINTR) {
+            /* A signal interrupted the wait. The sockets are untouched, so
+             * report an empty wait: the caller re-arms its timers and polls
+             * again, instead of ending the packet loop. */
+            *action = picoquic_packet_loop_action_timeout;
+        }
+        else {
+            bytes_recv = -1;
+            DBG_PRINTF("Error: poll returns %d, errno %d\n", ret_poll, errno);
+        }
     }
     else if (ret_poll == 0) {
         *action = picoquic_packet_loop_action_timeout;
